#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110400 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110771 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a2d7c38-0e96-5f81-81e3-d6acd0215463" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Variable Given an Exterior Angle and Two Internal Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "1642f15d-32c8-5a19-b20b-95f501dc0a97" :question/math-academy-id "e-2711" :question/problem "Find the value of $x$ in the following figure.\n\n![](images/e7/e74d124c971c55844381cf9cedaa0e392e634e5ceda31d6af7a136426678f4db.png)" :question/worked-solution "*Method 1*\n\nLet's start by finding the value of the missing interior angle at $B.$ We use the fact that the interior and exterior angles are supplementary:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{73}^{∘}+m∠B & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={180}^{∘}-{73}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={107}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $x$ using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {41}^{∘}+{107}^{∘}+2x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {148}^{∘}+2x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={32}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={16}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n*Method 2*\n\nWe could rewrite the equation $m\\angle{A} +m\\angle{B} +m\\angle{C} = 180^\\circ$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠B}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at [math]B[/math]}}\\,\\,\\,=m∠A+m∠C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠B}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at [math]B[/math]}}\\,\\,\\, & =m∠A+m∠C \\\\ {73}^{∘} & ={41}^{∘}+2x \\\\ {32}^{∘} & =2x \\\\ {16}^{∘} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110401 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110772 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba344d2f-aeb4-54ca-ab5d-e0abeceddba5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Variable Given the Exterior Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "d9bf29fd-5151-591f-b6af-8f5e53b3d231" :question/math-academy-id "e-2712" :question/problem "Find the value of $x$ in the figure below.\n\n![](images/6e/6eb087290fa6ea66325d35fa626ef385bb4d4e477e6e1c40057b9f9d7e43d9d4.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since the exterior angles of a triangle sum to $360^{\\circ}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{145}^{∘}+x+(2x-{25}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {120}^{∘}+3x & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & ={240}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={80}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110402 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110773 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66f215f1-b90c-5284-8628-6a94bf37de0a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Measure of an Interior Angle Given the Exterior Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "e97d1ab9-f0b2-5e27-afff-a84da69c7487" :question/math-academy-id "e-2714" :question/problem "Given a triangle $\\triangle ABC$, let the exterior angles at ${A}$ and ${B}$ be equal $120^\\circ$ and $100^\\circ,$ respectively. What is the measure of the angle $\\angle C?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by drawing the corresponding triangle.\n\n![](images/3e/3e2698fe8e2e886127669b1e04f45499706750312b447ff0c73524f2de4ff2ce.png)\n\n  \n\nLet $z$ be the measure of the exterior angle at ${C}$ pictured above. Since the sum of the exterior angles of a triangle is $360^{\\circ}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{120}^{∘}+{100}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {220}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={140}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the value of the missing interior angle at $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠C & ={180}^{∘}-z \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{140}^{∘} \\\\  & ={40}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $m\\angle C = 40^\\circ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110772 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba344d2f-aeb4-54ca-ab5d-e0abeceddba5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Variable Given the Exterior Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39fd7c9e-0ea0-51fd-96cb-49ea653f5205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{147}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505206} {:answer/id #uuid "5ca05c0c-36e6-59da-9e50-1878055139fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{155}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505292} {:answer/id #uuid "6868e3ac-2802-51a4-9858-c14971faab65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{150}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505315} {:answer/id #uuid "a34e5c5f-3c49-58e4-83e6-1303c1acda7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{145}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505429} {:answer/id #uuid "ae5901e4-7a89-5d9b-9062-e68459783c38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{153}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6868e3ac-2802-51a4-9858-c14971faab65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{150}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505315} :answer-field/id #uuid "b57106eb-7e3b-5bf6-86f0-3a76e4857040" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4267ffed-6129-5b8f-a3a0-b5586fcb6f27" :question/math-academy-id "q-116894" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3bfe01c879d52b6b0cb6dbde604b506a173885ffad258cd9fa52ec298d579201.png)\n\nFind the value of $x$ in the figure above." :question/worked-solution "Since the exterior angles of a triangle sum to ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{80}^{∘}+(x-{20}^{∘})+x & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 2x+{60}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={300}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={150}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110772 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba344d2f-aeb4-54ca-ab5d-e0abeceddba5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Variable Given the Exterior Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6afe23f2-c90b-52f4-ac6a-f4d826bab584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6afe23f2-c90b-52f4-ac6a-f4d826bab584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505321} :answer-field/id #uuid "81a624d5-131f-5c71-bc59-c8a7a244375c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0fca551-3459-553b-ab09-61f3dc19f41b" :question/math-academy-id "q-171881" :question/problem "![](images/07/07059a0b58535952d244533e58d513f4bf538b1e2f4237888f13cc4751dab893.png)\n\nIn the figure above, the value of $x$ is{{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the exterior angles of a triangle sum to ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x+(14x+{2}^{∘})+(12x+{18}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 34x+{20}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 34x & ={340}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={10}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110771 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a2d7c38-0e96-5f81-81e3-d6acd0215463" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Variable Given an Exterior Angle and Two Internal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a2e21a4-7ffd-5db7-81d7-cae99b955de3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{52}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505180} {:answer/id #uuid "56a0ec06-e947-55b9-8faf-8159c782df26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{51}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505279} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b536a0-bbdf-58b0-8b9c-5e24d6176bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{54}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505426} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d0ad7a-67ee-5424-8c2a-91055577258a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{57}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505522} {:answer/id #uuid "faea2663-5b85-5875-abac-c09b7800f930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{56}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1b536a0-bbdf-58b0-8b9c-5e24d6176bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{54}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505426} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac45f585-c55f-5b39-89b6-7c56493dc1bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a9f9e86-5147-5e3f-981f-c02eff224304" :question/math-academy-id "q-20681" :question/problem "Consider a triangle $△ABC$, where the measure of an exterior angle at $C$ is equal to ${127}^{∘}$ and $m∠B={73}^{∘}.$ What is the measure of the angle $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by drawing the corresponding triangle.\n\n![](images/07/0729bbdc2bec04cee7be1f0be31a44648b05a2536f7e17002d1dab6e2f3500c6.png)\n\n*Method 1*\n\nLet's start by finding the value of the missing interior angle at $C.$ We use the fact that the interior and exterior angles are supplementary:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{127}^{∘}+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠C & ={180}^{∘}-{127}^{∘} \\\\ m∠C & ={53}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $∠A$ using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A+{73}^{∘}+{53}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={180}^{∘}-{126}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={54}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n*Method 2*\n\nWe could rewrite the equation $m∠A+m∠B+m∠C={180}^{∘}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠C}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at }C}\\,\\,\\,=m∠A+m∠B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we can solve for $m∠A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠C}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at }C}\\,\\,\\, & =m∠A+m∠B \\\\ {127}^{∘} & =m∠A+{73}^{∘} \\\\ {127}^{∘}-{73}^{∘} & =m∠A \\\\ {54}^{∘} & =m∠A\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110771 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a2d7c38-0e96-5f81-81e3-d6acd0215463" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Variable Given an Exterior Angle and Two Internal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab03a6b-b7a6-52e5-a099-6438918f7051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505183} {:answer/id #uuid "366c1c43-d53f-58eb-8483-cd45c87c0df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{55}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505201} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0fe1f2-d6a9-56e5-a21a-230a23f1a790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{70}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505288} {:answer/id #uuid "80d042a4-ea23-5dfa-8c60-7d4079d61277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505363} {:answer/id #uuid "deb2bb45-fca9-5c51-bbd9-5d9ea3559395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "366c1c43-d53f-58eb-8483-cd45c87c0df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{55}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505201} :answer-field/id #uuid "91303600-5ad0-566f-a6d1-5d6adbbae40c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "582dce9c-b3c2-5822-8e23-d7a58cdba267" :question/math-academy-id "q-53028" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8bd85835b1bb951df5773824b45f53527a4cda3c4e988f7b30f151e289d47459.png)\n\nIn the figure shown above, we know that \n$m∠A=m∠B.$\nWhat is the measure of the angle $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the measure of the interior angle at $A.$ Then,\n\n$$\nm∠A=m∠B=x.\n$$\n\n*Method 1*\n\nLet's start by finding the value of the missing interior angle at $C.$ We use the fact that the interior and exterior angles are supplementary:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{110}^{∘}+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠C & ={180}^{∘}-{110}^{∘} \\\\ m∠C & ={70}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $x$ using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x+x+{70}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={110}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={55}^{∘}\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Method 2*\n\nWe could rewrite the equation $m∠A+m∠B+m∠C={180}^{∘}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠C}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at }C}\\,\\,\\,=m∠A+m∠B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we can solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠C}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at }C}\\,\\,\\, & =m∠A+m∠B \\\\ {110}^{∘} & =x+x \\\\ {110}^{∘} & =2x \\\\ {55}^{∘} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110773 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66f215f1-b90c-5284-8628-6a94bf37de0a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Measure of an Interior Angle Given the Exterior Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60d2dd30-298a-557e-bc40-f2d7d3620958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{46}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505300} {:answer/id #uuid "8af4d36e-fe88-5a74-8ad7-fe65d8abc1ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{38}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505375} {:answer/id #uuid "aacbffa7-8c20-5000-9115-ca5b2b62f092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{42}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505445} {:answer/id #uuid "af344a1a-674e-54cf-8766-1a8770d632bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505450} {:answer/id #uuid "e058fdbb-c7c1-5343-b0b2-46fb3b24d7b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aacbffa7-8c20-5000-9115-ca5b2b62f092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{42}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505445} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec105581-d8ab-5cfb-9904-990edbb43675" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0d1d413-28f9-5b6c-8380-80f5141c57c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-53049" :question/problem "Consider a triangle $△ABC$, where an exterior angle at $A$ is ${100}^{∘}$ and an exterior angle at $B$ is ${122}^{∘}.$ What is the measure of the interior angle $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a corresponding picture.\n\n![](images/b1/b1d04d90855560e36762d492f34ac14fb960c98f55c234da7c706e638fd9ea9e.png)\n\n   \n\nLet $z$ be the measure of the exterior angle at $C$ pictured above. Since the sum of the exterior angles of a triangle is ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{100}^{∘}+{122}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {222}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={138}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the value of the missing interior angle at $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠C & ={180}^{∘}-z \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{138}^{∘} \\\\  & ={42}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $m∠C={42}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110773 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66f215f1-b90c-5284-8628-6a94bf37de0a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Measure of an Interior Angle Given the Exterior Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "788dc8c4-399c-5d30-9a77-ac4615aee599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{42}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505349} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca2d62e-04c1-575d-8c73-e4320dfe57e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505378} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6c745e-2beb-5cc5-9aca-92fb40b3e598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{44}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505409} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fe4011-c9fd-5df4-a4d5-65f94cbda238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505496} {:answer/id #uuid "e0964060-909a-5441-84da-b4e0e78eafc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{38}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "788dc8c4-399c-5d30-9a77-ac4615aee599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{42}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505349} :answer-field/id #uuid "28a9e750-d735-5da4-935f-b98288512909" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a5dab59-2250-5c49-b3b0-a8075568ace9" :question/math-academy-id "q-77566" :question/problem "Consider a triangle $△ABC$, where an exterior angle at $A$ is ${119}^{∘}$ and an exterior angle at $C$ is ${103}^{∘}.$ What is the measure of the interior angle $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a corresponding picture.\n\n![](images/6f/6fc411e7715044257a6818fe63fb3b7bd212c22a2f3d279f282b2b7161be3ecc.png)\n\n   \n\nLet $z$ be the measure of the exterior angle at $B$ pictured above. Since the sum of the exterior angles of a triangle is ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{119}^{∘}+{103}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {222}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={138}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the value of the missing interior angle at $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠B & ={180}^{∘}-z \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{138}^{∘} \\\\  & ={42}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $m∠B={42}^{∘}.$"}]}
